**Question 8 (coding, 12 points)**

Write a function that returns, **in their original order**, all the words that appear **exactly once** in the input vector. For example, `{"red", "blue", "red", "green", "blue", "pink"}` gives `{"green", "pink"}`. Use an `std::unordered_map`.

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> uniqueWords(const std::vector<std::string>& words) {
    // write code here
}
```

(9 points for the code.) Then **(3 points)**: give the average-case and the worst-case running time in terms of n = words.size(), assuming each word has bounded length, and justify them.

questions.answer:
```cpp
std::vector<std::string> uniqueWords(const std::vector<std::string>& words) {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> count;
    for (const std::string& w : words)
        ++count[w];                      // operator[] creates the entry with 0, then ++ makes it 1
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    for (const std::string& w : words)   // second pass keeps the original order
        if (count[w] == 1)
            result.push_back(w);
    return result;
}
```

**Complexity.** Two passes over the n words, each doing one hash operation per word (`operator[]` in the first, a lookup in the second). Each hash operation is O(1) on average, so the function is **O(n) average** (two loops one after the other: n + n). In the worst case every word lands in the same bucket and each hash operation costs O(n), so the worst case is **O(n^2)**. (Iterating over the map instead of the vector would lose the original order.)

