**Question 9 (coding, 12 points)**

A task scheduler stores tasks as

```cpp
struct Task {
    std::string name;
    int priority;   // larger number = more urgent
    int arrival;    // smaller number = arrived earlier
};
```

Tasks must be run from the **highest priority** to the lowest; among tasks with the same priority the one that **arrived earliest** runs first. Using `std::priority_queue` (which is a max-heap), complete the code: write the comparison functor `Later`, and the function that returns the task names in the order they must run.

```cpp
struct Later {
    // write code here
};

std::vector<std::string> scheduleOrder(const std::vector<Task>& tasks) {
    // write code here
}
```

(9 points for the code.) Then **(3 points)**: give the tight worst-case running time of `scheduleOrder` for n tasks and justify it.

questions.answer:
```cpp
struct Later {
    // priority_queue keeps the LARGEST element (per this "less than") on top.
    // So "a < b" must mean "a should come out AFTER b".
    bool operator()(const Task& a, const Task& b) const {
        if (a.priority != b.priority)
            return a.priority < b.priority;   // lower priority comes out later
        return a.arrival > b.arrival;         // equal priority: later arrival comes out later
    }
};

std::vector<std::string> scheduleOrder(const std::vector<Task>& tasks) {
    std::priority_queue<Task, std::vector<Task>, Later> pq;
    for (const Task& t : tasks)
        pq.push(t);                           // n pushes, O(log n) each
    std::vector<std::string> order;
    while (!pq.empty()) {
        order.push_back(pq.top().name);       // top() is O(1)
        pq.pop();                             // pop() is O(log n)
    }
    return order;
}
```

**Complexity.** n `push` calls at O(log n) each = O(n log n); then n iterations each doing `top()` O(1), `pop()` O(log n), `push_back` amortized O(1) = O(n log n). The two loops are consecutive, so the total is **O(n log n)** worst case. (Building the heap with the range constructor would be O(n), but the n pops still cost O(n log n).)

Trap: `priority_queue` puts the element that is *greatest according to the comparator* on top, so the functor must say "a < b" when a should run **after** b. Returning `a.priority > b.priority` would make the lowest priority run first.

(`Task` is as declared in the question; the answer assumes `<queue>`, `<string>` and `<vector>` are included.)
